# Phase 23 - Edge States, Auth, Recharge, Records, About, and 404 Trust Polish Audit

Date: 2026-06-26
Scope: auth modal, auth-required states, generic loading/empty states, recharge flow, transaction history, about page, 404 page.
Goal lens: refined, breathable, simple. No hardcoding, no temporary CSS pressure, no page-by-page utility stacks.
Benchmark lens: X, Threads, Instagram, and Apple-style product polish: trust-critical states should feel as intentional as the main feed.
Evidence type: static source and style-contract audit. This phase does not claim fresh rendered browser validation.

## Files inspected

- `src/features/auth/AuthModal.tsx`
- `src/styles/system/ui-primitives-auth.css`
- `src/ui/AuthRequiredState.tsx`
- `src/ui/LoadingState.tsx`
- `src/ui/UIStateBlock.tsx`
- `src/ui/EmptyStateCard.tsx`
- `src/ui/ActionButton.tsx`
- `src/pages/RechargeMobile.tsx`
- `src/styles/features/recharge.css`
- `src/pages/TransactionHistoryMobile.tsx`
- `src/pages/BrandAbout.tsx`
- `src/styles/features/brand.css`
- `src/pages/NotFound.tsx`

Note: transaction record CSS owner was already covered in earlier promote/records passes; this phase focuses on current page composition and shared state contracts.

## Executive summary

The product has solid trust-state foundations. Auth-required, loading, state block, action button, recharge, and record pages all use reusable components or semantic classes in important places. That is the right direction.

The remaining issue is uneven polish at the edges. Login/register, recharge, records, about, and 404 are all trust-bearing surfaces, but they do not share one fully enforced state/page contract yet. AuthModal mixes semantic classes with arbitrary animation utilities and local spacing utilities. NotFound is still built mostly from inline utility classes. About has token-driven CSS, but the TSX still owns grid/margin/button sizing. EmptyStateCard leaks `py-12/py-16`, which is small but symbolically important: empty states should be a design-system primitive, not page padding trivia.

## Overall health

Score: 7.0 / 10

Strengths:

- `AuthRequiredState` is a reusable component and uses a semantic card surface: `src/ui/AuthRequiredState.tsx:29-37`.
- `LoadingState` centralizes loading, refresh, error retry, and state block patterns with ARIA status/live behavior: `src/ui/LoadingState.tsx:49-181`.
- `UIStateBlock` gives empty/error/success/info a single structure: icon, title, description, action, secondary action in `src/ui/UIStateBlock.tsx:34-63`.
- `ActionButton` maps variants and sizes to semantic button classes rather than local utility stacks: `src/ui/ActionButton.tsx:29-53`.
- Recharge page has a strong page owner CSS file and a mostly semantic class structure: `src/pages/RechargeMobile.tsx:378-539`, `src/styles/features/recharge.css:4-287`.
- Transaction history uses `StateBlock`, `EmptyStateCard`, `ListLoadMoreState`, `SurfaceSectionCard`, and `HeaderSelectAction`: `src/pages/TransactionHistoryMobile.tsx:244-350`.
- About page has a dedicated CSS owner and product-token-driven surfaces: `src/styles/features/brand.css:1-151`.

Primary risks:

- AuthModal is visually structured but still has arbitrary animation and utility spacing in TSX.
- 404 page bypasses the state system and is built from utility classes.
- About page layout still has grid/margin/button sizing in TSX.
- Empty states do not yet have a strict density/spacing contract.
- Recharge QR generation depends on a third-party URL with visual parameters embedded in code.

## P0 findings

### P0-1 - AuthModal has a good semantic base, but its highest-visibility polish is still partly utility-driven

Evidence:

- Auth overlay and panel use arbitrary animation classes: `animate-[fadeIn_160ms_ease-out]`, `animate-[sheet-pop-in_180ms_ease-out]` in `src/features/auth/AuthModal.tsx:195-205`.
- Tab content uses `animate-[fadeIn_140ms_ease-out]` in `src/features/auth/AuthModal.tsx:254-314`.
- Scroll/body spacing uses `overflow-y-auto px-6 py-7 text-center sm:px-8 sm:py-8` in `src/features/auth/AuthModal.tsx:217`.
- Forms use `space-y-3.5 text-left`, input rows use `flex items-center gap-3 p-1`, and submit buttons use `mt-2 h-12 w-full` in `src/features/auth/AuthModal.tsx:259-306` and `src/features/auth/AuthModal.tsx:314-437`.
- The CSS side already has real auth tokens/classes: `.ui-auth-tablist`, `.ui-auth-tab`, `.ui-auth-input`, `.ui-auth-agreement`, `.ui-auth-error`, and `.ui-auth-required-card` in `src/styles/system/ui-primitives-auth.css:147-456`.

Why this hurts refinement:

- Login/register is a trust-critical first impression. It should be fully contract-owned.
- Arbitrary animation utilities make motion feel like a local patch rather than a product behavior.
- Field spacing and submit height should match the form/control system used in profile, recharge, post-create, and promote.

Recommendation:

- Add AuthModal structure classes for body, form, field row, tab transition, and submit sizing.
- Replace arbitrary animation utilities with named motion classes: `ui-auth-scrim-enter`, `ui-auth-panel-enter`, `ui-auth-tab-panel-enter`.
- Promote auth field geometry into a shared form/input contract instead of `gap-3 p-1` and `h-12` utilities.

Acceptance gate:

- `src/features/auth/AuthModal.tsx` should contain no `animate-[...]`, no raw spacing utility stacks for form rhythm, and no direct height utilities for primary submit buttons.

### P0-2 - 404 page bypasses the shared empty/error state system

Evidence:

- NotFound uses a utility-built state block: `flex min-h-[45vh] flex-col items-center justify-center gap-4 text-center` in `src/pages/NotFound.tsx:20`.
- Icon container uses `flex h-14 w-14 items-center justify-center rounded-full ui-bg-weak` in `src/pages/NotFound.tsx:21`.
- Action row uses `flex gap-3` in `src/pages/NotFound.tsx:26`.
- The app already has `StateBlock`, `EmptyStateCard`, and `AuthRequiredState`, so this page does not need bespoke layout.

Why this hurts refinement:

- 404 is often a user’s recovery point after a failed share/link. It should feel calm and intentional.
- Utility-built 404 risks inconsistent vertical centering, icon shape, copy width, and button spacing.
- It undermines the shared state system that already exists.

Recommendation:

- Replace NotFound composition with `StateBlock` or a dedicated `NotFoundState` built on `UIStateBlock`.
- Add an `error/recovery` tone with icon slot, title, copy, primary action, secondary action.
- Move min-height and action gap into state contract tokens.

Acceptance gate:

- `src/pages/NotFound.tsx` should have no visual utility stacks beyond semantic state component usage.

### P0-3 - EmptyStateCard leaks page rhythm through `py-12/py-16`

Evidence:

- `EmptyStateCard` appends `py-12` or `py-16` based on compact mode in `src/ui/EmptyStateCard.tsx:29-33`.
- `UIStateBlock` and core CSS already own state block compact/inline classes.

Why this hurts refinement:

- Empty state vertical rhythm should be system-controlled, not Tailwind padding.
- The exact same empty state can feel too tall or too cramped across record lists, feed lists, profile lists, and modal sheets if the padding is hardcoded at wrapper level.

Recommendation:

- Add `ui-empty-state-card`, `ui-empty-state-card--compact`, and `ui-empty-state-card--comfortable` classes.
- Use density props mapped to tokens instead of `py-*` utilities.
- Consider `context="page|list|sheet|inline"` so empty states align with their container.

Acceptance gate:

- `EmptyStateCard` uses semantic density classes only.

### P0-4 - Recharge QR asset generation is code-owned and visually parameterized inline

Evidence:

- `getTronAddressQrUrl` builds `https://api.qrserver.com/v1/create-qr-code/?size=320x320&data=...&margin=16&color=111111&bgcolor=ffffff` in `src/pages/RechargeMobile.tsx:58-61`.
- QR visual sizing on the page is controlled by CSS tokens in `src/styles/features/recharge.css:237-254`.

Why this hurts refinement and trust:

- Recharge is a money-adjacent flow. The QR should be treated as a trusted asset/data surface.
- Visual parameters like QR size, margin, foreground, and background are hardcoded in business code.
- A third-party QR service URL affects reliability, privacy, and visual consistency.

Recommendation:

- Move QR URL generation and visual parameters into a payment/QR adapter or server endpoint.
- Expose a `PaymentQr` component that owns loading, empty, error, alt text, and retry/copy affordances.
- Store QR visual values as payment asset tokens or backend config, not inline strings.

Acceptance gate:

- Recharge page should not assemble third-party visual asset URLs inline.
- QR empty/error/loading states should be component-owned.

## P1 findings

### P1-1 - Recharge page is mostly semantic, but inline utility remnants should be folded into local contracts

Evidence:

- Busy button content uses `inline-flex items-center gap-2` in `src/pages/RechargeMobile.tsx:443-454` and `src/pages/RechargeMobile.tsx:519-531`.
- Token amount row uses `min-w-0 tabular-nums`, address uses `font-mono`, and icon sizes are passed inline in `src/pages/RechargeMobile.tsx:391-508`.
- Dedicated CSS already owns the page’s balance card, amount field, preset grid, token panel, QR card, address row, and copy button in `src/styles/features/recharge.css:20-287`.

Design read:

- This is not a broken page. It is close.
- The remaining utility classes are small, but recharge is trust-sensitive, so even small inconsistencies matter.

Recommendation:

- Add `.recharge-submit-content`, `.recharge-token-amount`, `.recharge-address-mono`, and `.recharge-balance-check-icon` classes.
- Use icon-size tokens instead of inline `size={22}`, `size={18}`, and `size={14}` where visual consistency matters.
- Consider a status explainer card for `WAITING_PAYMENT`, `MANUAL_REVIEW`, and `CREDITED` to reduce uncertainty.

Acceptance gate:

- Recharge TSX should primarily describe state and content; CSS should own final layout and visual rhythm.

### P1-2 - Transaction history state architecture is good, but filter and empty contexts need stronger explanatory copy

Evidence:

- Error state includes title, description, action, and error tone in `src/pages/TransactionHistoryMobile.tsx:265-281`.
- Empty state only renders `EmptyStateCard title="暂无记录"` in `src/pages/TransactionHistoryMobile.tsx:335-337`.
- Header filter is available through `HeaderSelectAction` in `src/pages/TransactionHistoryMobile.tsx:248-259`.

Why this matters:

- Record pages are accountability surfaces. Users want to understand whether no records means no spend, filtered-out results, or loading/data issue.
- A sparse empty state is clean, but it can feel uncertain when filters are active.

Recommendation:

- Empty copy should depend on filter state:
  - All: `暂无交易记录，充值和推广消费会显示在这里。`
  - Recharge: `暂无充值记录。生成付款信息后可在这里查看到账状态。`
  - Promotion: `暂无推广消费记录。`
- Add secondary action when useful: go recharge or reset filter.
- Keep this inside `EmptyStateCard`/`StateBlock`, not local layout.

Acceptance gate:

- Empty state copy changes by current filter context.
- Users can recover from over-filtering without guessing.

### P1-3 - About page has a token-driven CSS owner, but TSX still owns layout rhythm

Evidence:

- About page has a dedicated CSS file with token-driven hero, cards, chips, sections, FAQ, and responsive behavior in `src/styles/features/brand.css:4-151`.
- TSX still controls action button height/padding: `h-11 px-5` in `src/pages/BrandAbout.tsx:107-122`.
- TSX still controls layout grid/margins/gaps: `mt-4 grid grid-cols-1 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2`, `mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2`, `mt-4 grid gap-3`, and `flex items-start gap-3` in `src/pages/BrandAbout.tsx:128-176`.

Why this hurts refinement:

- About is a brand page. It should be one of the cleanest surfaces in the product.
- The CSS owner already exists, so keeping layout utilities in TSX creates split ownership.

Recommendation:

- Move feature grid, keyword list, reason list, FAQ spacing, and action button sizing into `brand.css`.
- Add semantic classes like `brand-about-feature-grid`, `brand-about-keyword-list`, `brand-about-reason-list`, `brand-about-action`.
- Keep TSX as content structure only.

Acceptance gate:

- `BrandAbout.tsx` contains no layout utility classes for margin, grid, gap, height, or padding.

### P1-4 - Auth agreement content is functionally complete, but accessibility semantics can improve

Evidence:

- Auth dialog has `role="dialog"`, `aria-modal="true"`, and `aria-labelledby="auth-modal-title"` in `src/features/auth/AuthModal.tsx:201-205`.
- Agreement link has `aria-expanded={isAgreementOpen}` in `src/features/auth/AuthModal.tsx:383-391`.
- Tab buttons use `aria-pressed` rather than tab semantics in `src/features/auth/AuthModal.tsx:227-251`.

Recommendation:

- Consider `role="tablist"`, `role="tab"`, `aria-selected`, and `aria-controls` if the UI behaves as tabs.
- Add `aria-controls` to the agreement toggle and an `id` to the agreement panel.
- Confirm focus is trapped within the modal, not just scroll locked.

Acceptance gate:

- Keyboard users can switch login/register, open agreement, collapse agreement, submit, and close without focus ambiguity.

## P2 findings

### P2-1 - Loading and refresh states are strong, but icon sizing should become tokenized

Evidence:

- `InlineSpinner` accepts raw numeric `size` with default `18` in `src/ui/LoadingState.tsx:49-60`.
- LoadingBlock chooses `18` or `22` based on compact state in `src/ui/LoadingState.tsx:80`.
- RefreshHint uses raw `14` icon sizes in `src/ui/LoadingState.tsx:169-173`.

Recommendation:

- Replace numeric icon sizes with semantic sizes: `xs`, `sm`, `md`, `lg` mapped to icon tokens.
- Keep raw numeric override only as an escape hatch.

### P2-2 - ActionButton has variants/sizes, but no density language for trust pages

Evidence:

- `ActionButtonSize` only includes `md`, `sm`, and `header` in `src/ui/ActionButton.tsx:6-41`.
- Auth and about pages pass direct height/padding utilities for important actions.

Recommendation:

- Add semantic sizes for `fullWidth`, `sheetPrimary`, `trustPrimary`, or use a `block` prop plus density classes.
- This would remove `h-12 w-full`, `h-11 px-5`, and similar repeated local decisions.

## Benchmark mapping

### X / Threads

- Edge states should be calm, text-led, and recoverable.
- Record and error states should tell users what happened and what to do next.
- Current fit: `StateBlock` and `ActionButton` are aligned; sparse empty copy needs improvement.

### Instagram

- Auth and recovery screens should feel as visually disciplined as feed/profile.
- Current fit: Auth has brand lockup and structured agreement; 404 is not yet aligned.

### Apple

- Trust flows need predictable controls, consistent focus, and semantic motion.
- Current fit: safe-area and reusable state foundations exist; motion/field spacing still needs named contracts.

## Recommended contracts

### TrustState Contract

Required owners:

- Page-level empty/error/recovery layout.
- Icon shape and size.
- Title/copy/action spacing.
- Primary and secondary action row behavior.
- Context-aware empty copy.
- ARIA role/live behavior.

Suggested API:

```ts
<StateBlock tone="error" context="page" density="comfortable" primaryAction secondaryAction />
```

### AuthModal Contract

Required owners:

- Overlay enter/exit motion.
- Panel geometry.
- Body scroll padding.
- Tab switch motion and semantics.
- Field row geometry.
- Submit action sizing.
- Agreement panel spacing and accessibility wiring.

### PaymentQr Contract

Required owners:

- QR data source.
- QR visual parameters.
- Loading/empty/error display.
- Copy/retry affordance.
- Accessibility label.

### BrandPage Contract

Required owners:

- Hero rhythm.
- Feature grid.
- Keyword chips.
- Reason list.
- FAQ list.
- CTA sizing.

## Implementation sequence

1. Convert NotFound to shared `StateBlock`/`TrustState` composition.
2. Remove `py-12/py-16` from `EmptyStateCard` and introduce semantic density classes.
3. Move AuthModal animation, body spacing, form rhythm, and submit sizing into named classes.
4. Move BrandAbout layout utilities into `brand.css` semantic classes.
5. Fold recharge inline utility remnants into recharge CSS classes.
6. Add context-aware empty copy/actions to transaction history.
7. Create `PaymentQr` component or adapter for QR source and visual parameters.
8. Replace raw icon sizes in loading/trust states with semantic icon size props.

## Release-readiness gates

Static gates:

- `NotFound.tsx` has no visual utility layout stack.
- `EmptyStateCard.tsx` has no Tailwind padding utilities.
- `AuthModal.tsx` has no `animate-[...]` classes.
- `BrandAbout.tsx` has no layout utility classes for grid/gap/margin/height/padding.
- Recharge TSX has no inline submit content layout utilities.
- Trust-state icon sizes are tokenized.

Rendered gates:

- Login, register, agreement expanded, auth-required, recharge amount, recharge QR, record empty, record error, about, and 404 all share one calm visual rhythm.
- Narrow mobile width does not produce cramped auth forms or awkward 404 button wrapping.
- Empty states explain next action clearly.
- Recharge QR, address, copy, and status states remain legible and trustworthy.

Accessibility gates:

- Auth dialog focus trap and tab semantics are validated.
- Agreement toggle has a controlled panel relationship.
- Record error state announces retry affordance clearly.
- 404 recovery actions are keyboard reachable and visually focused.
- Reduced motion disables auth/sheet enter motion.

## Current-state conclusion

The edge-state foundation is promising. Recharge and records are mostly on the right semantic path, and the shared state components are useful.

The product will feel materially more premium if the team treats edge states as first-class product surfaces: NotFound should use the same state system, AuthModal should stop carrying arbitrary motion/spacing utilities, About should move remaining layout into its CSS owner, and empty/loading/payment states should use named trust contracts. This is exactly the layer that turns a good-looking app into one that feels consistently designed.
